1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,800
Sunday night.

2
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,720
Bo's garage is now a headquarters.

3
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,460
There's a whiteboard, a laptop, five folding chairs,

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,500
and a bag of crisps nobody is allowed

5
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:08,060
to touch.

6
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:09,740
Nia lands in the driveway.

7
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:11,600
Ravi walks in with his headphones on.

8
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,460
Bo is already there, because it's her garage.

9
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:15,540
Kai has been watching the rock every night

10
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:16,080
for a week.

11
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:17,720
Now Kai has numbers.

12
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:19,380
Kai turns the laptop around.

13
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,080
On the screen there's a dot, and a

14
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:22,520
long curved line.

15
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:24,320
The line ends at Earth.

16
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,940
It's about a hundred and fifty metres wide,

17
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:27,460
Kai says.

18
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,880
And it's heading for us.

19
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,380
Nobody says anything.

20
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:31,740
Ravi takes off his headphones.

21
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:33,060
That's how you know it's serious.

22
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,820
So what if an asteroid was heading for

23
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:36,060
Earth?

24
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:36,920
A real one.

25
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:38,200
With your name on it.

26
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:39,720
Kai explains.

27
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:42,200
Every night for a week, Kai photographed the

28
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,180
same patch of sky.

29
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:44,760
The stars stay in the same place.

30
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,400
But one dot moved a little every night.

31
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,100
That's how real astronomers find asteroids.

32
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,380
They compare pictures of the sky, and look

33
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:53,400
for the dot that moves.

34
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:55,440
And this dot isn't just moving.

35
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:56,980
It's getting brighter.

36
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:58,920
Because it's getting closer.

37
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:01,480
First, the speed.

38
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,300
Asteroids hit Earth at about twenty kilometres a

39
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:04,640
second.

40
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,160
That's from London to Paris in under twenty

41
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:07,680
seconds.

42
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,940
And speed is where the energy hides.

43
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:12,360
Double the speed, and you get four times

44
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:12,980
the energy.

45
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,820
A rock this size, at that speed, carries

46
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:17,980
about a hundred and seventy megatons.

47
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:20,480
That's more than three times the biggest bomb

48
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:21,900
humans ever tested.

49
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,660
Bo cracks her knuckles.

50
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:25,200
Kai shakes their head.

51
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:26,840
Not even you.

52
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:29,840
Kai opens a video on the laptop.

53
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:32,320
Even small rocks are scary.

54
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:35,100
In 2013, over the city of Chelyabinsk in

55
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,760
Russia, a rock about eighteen metres across hit

56
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:38,040
the air.

57
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:39,820
That's about the length of two buses.

58
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:42,860
It exploded above the city with about thirty

59
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:44,820
times the energy of the Hiroshima bomb.

60
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:46,140
Nobody saw it coming.

61
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:48,600
Around fifteen hundred people were hurt.

62
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:50,400
Almost all of them by glass, when the

63
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:51,580
shock wave blew in their windows.

64
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:53,540
People ran to their windows to see the

65
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:53,880
flash.

66
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,460
Then the windows came to them.

67
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:58,740
So the first rule of asteroids: if you

68
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:00,580
see a flash in the sky, stay away

69
00:02:00,580 --> 00:02:01,100
from the windows.

70
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:04,940
Go back to 1908, to a forest in

71
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:06,360
Siberia called Tunguska.

72
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:08,920
Something exploded in the air above it.

73
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:09,900
It left no crater.

74
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:13,440
Just eighty million trees, knocked flat, over an

75
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:14,820
area bigger than a big city.

76
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:17,360
And in Arizona, there's a hole more than

77
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,280
a kilometre wide.

78
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,280
It was made about fifty thousand years ago,

79
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,520
by a lump of iron about fifty metres

80
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:23,120
across.

81
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:24,820
Smaller than our rock.

82
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,360
And sixty-six million years ago, a rock about

83
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,840
ten kilometres wide hit what is now Mexico.

84
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:32,700
You know how that ended for the dinosaurs.

85
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:35,180
Ravi asks the obvious question.

86
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,320
How did nobody see that one coming?

87
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:38,760
Easy, Kai says.

88
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,000
Nobody had telescopes.

89
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:42,600
Today, people do.

90
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:45,140
Astronomers watch the sky every night, and they

91
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,240
track thousands of rocks.

92
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:49,100
On the thirteenth of April, 2029, an asteroid

93
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,860
called Apophis will fly past Earth closer than

94
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:52,640
some of our satellites.

95
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:53,600
It won't hit.

96
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:54,160
We checked.

97
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:54,720
Twice.

98
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,600
On that night, people in parts of the

99
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:58,000
world will be able to see it without

100
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:58,540
a telescope.

101
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,680
A real asteroid, sliding across the sky.

102
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:04,020
And NASA is building a space telescope called

103
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:06,040
NEO Surveyor, to find most of the big

104
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:07,080
ones before they find us.

105
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:08,580
But our rock?

106
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:10,620
Nobody has spotted it.

107
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:11,360
Except Kai.

108
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,180
Back in the garage, everybody has an idea.

109
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:15,620
Bo wants to punch it.

110
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:17,280
Nia wants to fly up and look at

111
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:17,500
it.

112
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:19,120
Ravi wants to try pushing it.

113
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:21,160
Kai reminds Nia that there's no air up

114
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:23,200
there, and it's much colder than her worst

115
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,580
day at three kilometres.

116
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:25,760
Nia sits back down.

117
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,620
Bo suggests a really big net.

118
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:29,100
Kai doesn't even answer.

119
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:31,500
And then you remember something you read.

120
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:35,060
In 2022, NASA crashed a spacecraft into an

121
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:36,060
asteroid on purpose.

122
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:37,180
It was called DART.

123
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:39,200
It hit a rock about the same size

124
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:42,420
as ours, at twenty-two thousand kilometres an hour.

125
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:44,780
And it changed the rock's orbit by thirty-two

126
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:45,240
minutes.

127
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:48,200
Humans have already moved an asteroid, you say.

128
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:49,640
So we can too.

129
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:52,100
Kai looks at you for a long time.

130
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:54,620
Then Kai writes one word on the whiteboard.

131
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:55,700
Nudge.

132
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:58,960
So what would you do if you saw

133
00:03:58,960 --> 00:03:59,280
it coming?

134
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:00,440
Tell us in the comments.

135
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,960
And subscribe, because next time, the five of

136
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:04,860
you try to move a rock from space.

137
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:06,900
And you find out what that would really

138
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:07,260
take.
